'''Navajas''' (also spelled '''Nabajaz'''), is an extensive [[Communes of Balisca|barrio]] in the [[Limón]] borough of the [[Castejón Metropolis]]. In the [[Baliscan Revolution|post-Revolution]] years, the [[Metropolitan Council of Castejón|communal government]] choose Limón to construct large amounts of {{W|social housing}} estates, creating a large amount of vacant housing units by the mid-1960s. Navajas is considered by many to be a major center of modern Baliscan popular culture and a major destination for followers of numerous {{W|subculture}}s, such as {{W|foodies}}, {{W|Video game culture|video gamers}}, {{W|maker culture|DIYers}}, fans of {{W|anime}} and {{W|manga}}, [[Sanese Drill]] and {{W|street wear}}. As a result of this concentration of subcultures, the barrio has has gained the nickname "Navajas Electric Town".

After the economic crisis and [[October 76|October 76 events]], the low-cost vacant housing made the area appealing to undocumented refugees and followers of emerging {{W|counterculture of the 1960s|counterculture movement}}s, and it became a center of the "[[Desconexión]]" movement. Studio apartments became home for families of four and five people and became what a local [[Mozos de Escuadra|''mozo'']] called "vertical villages". Simultaneously, this area saw a large increase in the number of {{W|cooperatives}} to supply clothes, {{W|food cooperative|food}}, household goods and associated items (often {{W|DIY#subculture|DIY}} made or from the {{W|black market}} if more complex), as well a large number of tailors and {{W|red light district|red light}} establishments such as {{W|love hotel}}s and {{W|bathhouse}}s. A substantial number of neighborhood's immigrant founded restaurants, coffee shops, ethnic grocery stores, video shops, and other stores that opened at this time still operate as the barrio resists {{W|gentrification}} and prohibits {{W|chain store}}s.

Every October, [[Navajas Communal Village Festival]] takes place in and around the area, with the focal point being on [[? Street]], where live music performances are held and international food markets take place. The term "{{W|Intentional community|Communal Village}}" originated with the formation of the [[Navajas Commune]] during the spring of 1976 as a response to the convergence of refugees, marginalized communities, and vulnerable young people on the Navajas district. The collective sought to alleviate some of the problems anticipated from the influx of people expected during the summer. The cooperative also assisted the Free Clinic and organized housing, food, sanitation, music and arts, along with maintaining coordination with local churches and other social groups. As a result, the district progressively went from being a marginal space to an open cosmopolitan one, without losing part of the original [[w:es:Chispero|''castizo'']] atmosphere. 

